{"name":"Tara","hex":"#def1dd","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Tara","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["An Irish place name, associated with the Hill of Tara, seat of ancient Irish kings.","A female given name transferred from the place name, of 20th century and later usage."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["One of the Mahavidyas.","The wife of Brihaspati, guru and priest of the Devas."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Tara"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tara (2001 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Tara is a 2001 film, known in the United States as Hood Rat. Directed by Leslie Small, it was released direct-to-video, and was poorly received. It focuses on a soft homeless man who befriends a rat he names Tara."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tara_(2001_film)"},{"title":"Tara (Buddhism)","blocks":[{"text":"Tara, Ārya Tārā, also known as Jetsün Dölma, is an important female Buddha in Buddhism, especially revered in Vajrayana Buddhism and Mahayana Buddhism. She may appear as a female bodhisattva in Mahayana Buddhism. In Vajrayana Buddhism, Green Tara is a female Buddha who is a consort of Amoghasiddhi Buddha. Tārā is also known as a saviouress who hears the cries of beings in saṃsāra and saves them from worldly and spiritual danger."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Old_Green_Tara.JPG/330px-Old_Green_Tara.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tara_(Buddhism)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Old_Green_Tara.JPG/500px-Old_Green_Tara.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Old_Green_Tara.JPG/1280px-Old_Green_Tara.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Old_Green_Tara.JPG